<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>With your phone, and without trusting it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What an app can see, what it can ask for, and the device screen that has the last word.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="by-name">It appears by name, and <span class="highlight">three screens</span> see its traffic at once.</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">skyBlip Go+ 00012A</span>: the product, then the
        24-bit address it got when it was <%= link_to "activated", briefing_activation_path %>.
        Nothing to pair, no code to type. It sends traffic as a FLARM
        NMEA data stream over both Bluetooth serial connection types that navigation software
        looks for, so the app you already fly with finds it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tablet on the yoke, the phone in a pocket and the passenger&rsquo;s
        iPad all see the same traffic. Only one app at a time can change
        settings: the first app to save settings keeps that right until it
        disconnects. The second app is told which app holds it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo">
      <%= content_image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.avif", alt: "A skyBlip Go in a white case with its antenna and the traffic radar on its e-paper screen" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Connect without pairing, a code or an account</li>
    <li>Use three connections at once</li>
    <li>Connect through Nordic UART or FLARM-style profiles</li>
    <li>Use SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and the rest</li>
    <li>The device keeps your latest flights</li>
    <li>Download them over the same link on the ground</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="asked">Two presses allow, <span class="highlight">one press refuses</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    When an app asks for something that touches the device you have on board,
    new firmware, a restart into the USB bootloader, a settings write, erasing
    your flights, switching it off, the device does not take the phone at its
    word. It puts the question on its own glass, names it, and waits thirty
    seconds:
    <span class="mono">PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW</span>,
    <span class="mono">ONE PRESS REFUSES</span>. No answer within that time
    counts as a refusal.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "confirm.png", alt: "The authorisation prompt: AUTHORISE, SETTINGS, apply the settings the phone sent, PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW over ONE PRESS REFUSES, refused after 30 s" %>
    <figcaption>The screen asks before saving settings</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="lede">
    Your callsign is one of those settings. The app sends it. The screen asks
    for permission. Once approved, it is shown on the status screen, broadcast
    once every ten seconds and shown on nearby aircraft&rsquo;s screens. You can also type it on the
    device itself, in <%= link_to_page "controls", label: "the callsign field", anchor: "callsign" %>,
    or set it from a browser in <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>, beside
    the aircraft type, the units and the alarm.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Presses only count once the question is on screen and you have stopped
    pressing: a series of presses used to change a setting cannot also
    authorise a request. The pad does nothing while a question is on screen.
  </p>

  <h3 id="firmware">New firmware comes <span class="highlight">from a web page</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %> sends a signed
    firmware file over the same Bluetooth link, from Chrome or Edge. The screen asks for permission
    once and names the version before the device accepts the file and installs it. The new
    firmware must then start its radio, receive data from its receiver and update its
    screen before it is kept. If it cannot, the device returns to the
    previous firmware and the settings you had when the update began.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    If the device cannot read your settings, it tells you. It uses
    its defaults. The self test&rsquo;s <span class="mono">STORAGE</span> row
    reads <span class="mono">NVS+NOR DEFAULTS</span>. The phone is told:
    check the callsign and the aircraft type before you fly.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    When the firmware will not take at all, the same page hands the device to
    its USB bootloader, after the same two presses. The device switches itself
    off with the screen shown below. The next press starts the bootloader: connected by a USB
    cable, the device appears as a drive named <span class="mono">TECHOBOOT</span>.
    Copy a <span class="mono">.uf2</span> file onto it to install new firmware.
    <span class="mono">RST</span> returns to skyBlip. Nothing transmits
    during recovery. Two presses of <span class="mono">RST</span> open the same
    drive without a phone.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "recovery.png", alt: "The recovery page: RECOVERY, USB BOOTLOADER, PRESS THE BUTTON TO START IT, then a drive named TECHOBOOT on USB. Copy a .uf2 file there to install new firmware. RST returns to skyBlip. Nothing transmits during recovery" %>
    <figcaption>The device is ready for USB recovery</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="no-pairing">Why there is no pairing.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Bluetooth pairing encrypts the data fields known as GATT characteristics. This
    causes a known Web Bluetooth failure on Windows and would break
    <%= link_to "the browser page that installs firmware", briefing_root_path %>.
    Security therefore relies on checking the firmware file, not on securing the link.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The bootloader checks the firmware signature against our key</li>
    <li>It refuses firmware older than the installed version</li>
    <li>An open transport cannot install firmware</li>
    <li>All other requests can only run on the ground</li>
    <li>The device refuses them unless you confirm on its screen</li>
    <li>Your presence at the device replaces a pairing code</li>
  </ul>
</section>

